{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Better anger.</b> The scholars of demonstrations have already explained that there are in man three souls. The first is the vegetative soul, and the matter is that just as among the species of plants and grasses and every tree there is a power whose name is soul, or whatever you wish, that grows the body of the plant and raises it and expands it, so too there is in man. And this soul prevails until a fixed time, and this soul is the one that desires and needs to eat. And the second soul is the animal soul, and it possesses the five senses, and possesses locomotion that goes from place to place, and this soul too is in man. And to man alone belongs a third soul, which is called neshamah; it is the speaking one, which distinguishes between true and false, possessor of wisdom. And the second soul is intermediate between the two souls." "And Elohim implanted intellect in man — it is called heart — to fulfill the desire of every soul in its time, and also aided him with commandments; through them all things stand in their proper arrangement. And this book is not suited to speak about the secret of the soul, for it is deep, and no understanding person can grasp the truth except after reading many books. And had I not needed to mention the parts of the souls for the purpose of my commentary on the verses, I would not have hinted at all, neither in general nor in particular. And the main need is that they will see in the words of Solomon in this book difficult sayings: in many places he says a thing and says its opposite. Because of this the Sages of Israel, may their memory be for a blessing, said: The Sages sought to store away the book of Ecclesiastes because its words contradict one another. He said, \"Anger is better than laughter,\" and its opposite: \"Anger rests in the bosom of fools.\" And likewise, \"In much wisdom is much anger,\" and its opposite: \"Remove anger from your heart.\" And likewise, \"It is good that one should eat and drink well,\" and its opposite: \"It is better to go to a house of mourning.\" And likewise, \"I praised joy,\" and its opposite: \"What does joy accomplish?\" And likewise, \"What advantage has the wise man over the fool,\" and its opposite: \"There is advantage to wisdom.\" And likewise, \"I praised the dead,\" and its opposite: \"A living dog is better.\" And likewise, \"There is no deed or reckoning or knowledge or wisdom in Sheol,\" and its opposite: \"There is a time for every desire.\" And likewise, \"It shall not be well with the wicked, nor shall he lengthen his days,\" and its opposite: \"There is a wicked man who prolongs his evil.\" And likewise, \"It shall be well with those who fear Elohim,\" and its opposite: \"There are wicked men to whom it happens according to the deeds of the righteous.\" And the one who searches well will find more such things in this book. And it is known that the least of the wise would not compose a book and hide his words in it. And one of the commentators was compelled to interpret the word \"Ecclesiastes\" like \"the assembly of Jacob,\" and said that his disciples composed the book, each one speaking according to his own thought — and this is not correct at all. Moreover, Ecclesiastes was wise, and behold he is one man. And further, \"Ecclesiastes sought to find words of delight,\" and the decisive proof: \"I, Ecclesiastes, was king.\" And after Scripture testified about Solomon that after him no wise man like him would arise, we know that there is no contradiction in his words; rather they are all straightforward to the one who understands and upright to those who find knowledge. And now I shall interpret them briefly. Even though the three souls are called by one name because of their unification — for the neshamah is called both ruach and nefesh — therefore I shall assign names to them so as not to elaborate in describing each one. The name of the soul that desires to eat and to rejoice and the lust of intercourse shall be nefesh. And the name of the soul that possesses sensation, that seeks dominion and greatness, shall be ruach. And the name of the soul of wisdom shall be neshamah. Thus too divided them our master Saadia the Gaon, may his memory be for a blessing. And it is known that when the nefesh prevails, the neshamah weakens and has no strength to stand before it, because it is body, and all its instincts aid it. Therefore one who engages in eating and drinking will never become wise. And when the neshamah joins with the ruach, they will defeat the nefesh; then the eyes of the neshamah will open somewhat to understand the wisdoms of the nations — but it cannot know the supreme wisdoms, because of the power of the ruach that seeks dominion, and it is what produces anger. Now this is the interpretation of \"anger is better than laughter.\" And the meaning of \"by the sadness of the countenance the heart is made good\": that is worry, as in \"why is your countenance fallen?\" And the meaning of \"heart\" is the intellect. And after the neshamah prevails over the nefesh with the help of the ruach, the neshamah must engage in wisdom, which will help it until it defeats the ruach and brings it under knowledge. This is the interpretation of \"Do not be hasty in your ruach to be angry.\" And the meaning of \"anger rests in the bosom of fools\": it is with them always and does not depart from them; but with the wise man it is found only in its time, as needed. And the meaning of \"in much wisdom is much anger\": like the matter of \"Do not be overly wise,\" for much wisdom increases one's anger over the vanities of the world and over the people of the world, and one departs from settlement, like the matter of \"why should you be desolate?\" and die before one's time. Therefore it is good for a man to weigh all his words and affairs in the scales of justice, and to give a portion to every soul in its time. And the meaning of \"Remove anger from your heart\" is: do not let the ruach rule over you. And likewise, \"Remove evil from your flesh\" — that is desire, for if one eats every food he desires, he brings evil upon his flesh; and likewise if he adds to intercourse, he brings sickly evil upon his flesh. And the meaning of \"flesh\" is his body. Now in this verse he has mentioned that a man should weaken, with all his ability, the ruach — possessor of anger — and the nefesh — possessor of desire. And the meaning of \"It is good that one should eat and drink well\": this is against the fool who toils to gather money and does not enjoy it. \"It is better to go to a house of mourning\": that is the truth. And the meaning of \"What advantage has the wise man\": if the wise man does as the fool's deed, since his soul is not satisfied, what advantage does he have? And that \"there is advantage to wisdom\": that is the truth. And likewise, \"I praised joy\": because the weary ones of the world who do not seek wisdom — the joyful ones are more praiseworthy than those who worry about mourning. And \"What does joy accomplish\": that is the truth. And the interpretation of \"I praised the dead\": that is against the oppressed. And the meaning of \"A living dog is better\": these are the words of the children of men. And it is also written above: \"Also the heart of the children of men is full of evil and madness in their heart,\" and likewise, \"There is no deed or reckoning\": these are their words. And the meaning of \"There is a time for every desire\": that is the truth. And the meaning of \"It shall not be well with the wicked, nor shall he lengthen his days\": it speaks according to the majority. And the meaning of \"There is a wicked man\": this is found occasionally, in few instances. And I have already mentioned at the beginning of the book that on account of a little evil it is not in the supernal wisdom to withhold much good — and like this in the book of Proverbs: he says, \"He who gathers by labor will increase,\" and that is the truth; and \"There is one who scatters and yet adds more,\" and that is the minority. And likewise, \"There are wicked men to whom it happens according to the deeds of the righteous.\" You will find in this book a thing and a second time where he appears to say the opposite. You will also find it in the book of Proverbs, like \"Do not answer a fool\" / \"Answer a fool.\" Also in the words of the Prophets and in the Torah of our Elohim, which is the main thing: \"However, there shall be no needy among you\" / \"The needy shall not cease.\" And all of them are true."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {1 "Several biblical verse quotations are given in abbreviated or paraphrased form (e.g. 'He who gathers by labor will increase' for Prov. 13:11); rendered as they stand in the Hebrew text. The phrase 'the wisdoms of the nations' (חכמות הגויות) may be a corruption or non-standard usage; translated literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:29.251274949Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}